---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import { site, practices } from '../data/site';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '../lib/schema';

/* NOTE ON THIS PAGE'S CONTENT.

   Everything stated here is drawn from the brief: what TGM Group does, the two
   practices, Trevor's name and role, and the contact details. His professional
   background, qualifications, years in practice and prior roles are NOT in the
   brief, so they are not on this page. A consulting site lives or dies on
   whether the founder is credible, and that section is the single most
   valuable thing still missing — it is marked as a placeholder rather than
   filled with a plausible-sounding career.

   The registration details are the same: the CoR15.1A is in the company
   folder but the registration number was not supplied in a readable form.
   Placeholders, not guesses. */
---

<Base
  title="About TGM Group: who we are and how we work"
  description="TGM Group is a South African business transformation and growth partner founded by Trevor G. Menyatso, delivering through two practices: TGM Business Services and TGM Business Analytics."
  jsonLd={[breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'About', href: '/about/' }])]}
>
  <PageHero
    accent="navy"
    title="A transformation partner that stays past the diagnosis"
    lead="TGM Group helps South African organisations improve how they operate, solve the problems underneath the symptoms, adopt AI and automation where it earns its cost, and grow in a way the business can absorb."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap about-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h2 class="t-h2 section-title">What we are</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          TGM Group is a business transformation and growth partner working with
          organisations in South Africa. The work divides into two practices: one focused on how a business runs, the other on what it can see about itself. Most engagements involve both.
        </p>
        <p>
          The position is narrow on purpose. We are not a generalist agency and
          we do not sell technology. We work on the operating layer: the
          processes, the decision rights, the reporting and the data that
          determine whether a strategy turns into anything.
        </p>
        <p>
          We also stay. The common failure of consulting is a well-argued
          recommendation delivered to a team with no capacity to implement it.
          Our engagement model has four phases and implementation is two of
          them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section practices-section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="rule rule-teal" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <h2 class="t-h2 section-title">Two practices, one group</h2>
      <p class="t-lead practices-intro">
        Separate capabilities, one engagement. You deal with one team and one
        scope, not two suppliers coordinating with each other.
      </p>

      <div class="practice-grid">
        {
          practices.map((practice) => (
            <article class:list={['practice-card', `practice-${practice.accent}`]}>
              <h3 class="t-h3">{practice.name}</h3>
              <p class="t-body-sm practice-body">{practice.description}</p>
              <ul class="practice-list">
                {practice.focus.map((item) => (
                  <li class="t-body-sm">{item}</li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap about-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h2 class="t-h2 section-title">{site.founder}</h2>
        <p class="t-caption founder-role">{site.founderRole}</p>
      </div>

      <div class="founder-body">
        <p class="t-lead founder-lead">
          Trevor leads every engagement personally. The diagnosis is his work,
          not a junior's, and he is in the room for the implementation.
        </p>

        {/* Every claim below is evidenced by a document Trevor supplied: the
             Elev8 Group reference letter and his CV. Nothing is rounded up.
             The certifications are described as in progress because that is
             what they are, and the practice's age is stated rather than
             skirted. If any of it changes, change it here. */}

        <p class="t-body">
          His background is data. He started on a call centre floor, moved into
          workforce management, where he forecast call volumes and built the
          schedules a floor of agents runs on, and then into reporting and
          analytics, building the Power BI dashboards a business actually makes
          its decisions from. A reference from Elev8 Group credits that
          reporting work with a 25% improvement in reporting efficiency.
        </p>

        <p class="t-body">
          He is self-taught. Between January 2022 and February 2023 he worked
          full time at learning the craft: Python, SQL and database management,
          data modelling and DAX, Power BI and Tableau. He is currently working
          through the Microsoft certifications that formalise it, DP-900,
          PL-300 and DP-700.
        </p>

        <p class="t-body">
          That route matters for this work. Operational problems show up in the
          numbers long before they reach a strategy document, and they show up
          first to the person building the report, setting the schedule and
          handling the exceptions. Trevor has spent his working life on that
          side of the business rather than describing it from the outside.
        </p>

        <p class="t-body">
          TGM Group was registered in August 2025, so the practice is young and
          the track record is still being built. He would rather you judged the
          work on the quality of the diagnosis than on a list of logos. That is
          what the discovery call is for: ninety minutes, no charge, and a
          straight answer about whether he can actually help you.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section second-person">
    <div class="wrap about-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="rule rule-teal" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <h2 class="t-h2 section-title">{site.clientRelationships.name}</h2>
        <p class="t-caption founder-role">{site.clientRelationships.role}</p>
      </div>

      <div class="founder-body">
        {/* Written from the biography Trevor supplied. Every fact is his: the
             roles, the Head of Administration and Strategy post, and both
             degrees. What is not carried over is the adjective run that came
             with it, describing him as motivated and versatile with strong
             analytical, communication, leadership and problem-solving
             abilities. On a site whose evidence section is headed "Evidence,
             not adjectives", that paragraph would have undercut the argument
             two screens above it. Nothing invented, nothing substantive cut. */}

        <p class="t-lead founder-lead">
          Siyabonga is who you deal with between sessions. He is also one of the
          two directors and the company's registered information officer.
        </p>

        <p class="t-body">
          His background is administration, operations and strategy. He has
          served as Head of Administration and Strategy, coordinating
          administrative functions, supporting strategic planning, working on
          the processes underneath both, and contributing to organisational
          growth. Before that, team leadership and customer service.
        </p>

        <p class="t-body">
          He holds a BA in Politics and International Relations and a BA Honours
          in Development Studies, and he is committed to continuous learning and
          to work that contributes something real to organisations and the
          communities they serve.
        </p>

        <p class="t-body">
          The pairing is deliberate. Trevor works on what the numbers say.
          Siyabonga works on whether the organisation around them can absorb the
          change. Most of what goes wrong in a transformation sits between those
          two things.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section details-section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="rule rule-navy" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <h2 class="t-h2 section-title">Company details</h2>

      <dl class="details">
        <div class="detail-row">
          <dt class="t-caption detail-key">Registered name</dt>
          <dd class="t-body-sm detail-value">{site.legalName}</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="detail-row">
          <dt class="t-caption detail-key">Registration number</dt>
          <dd class="t-body-sm detail-value">{site.registrationNumber}</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="detail-row">
          <dt class="t-caption detail-key">Registered</dt>
          <dd class="t-body-sm detail-value">
            {site.registrationDate}, {site.companyType}
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="detail-row">
          <dt class="t-caption detail-key">Directors</dt>
          <dd class="t-body-sm detail-value">{site.directors.join(', ')}</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="detail-row">
          <dt class="t-caption detail-key">VAT number</dt>
          <dd class="t-body-sm detail-value">
            <span class="placeholder">[VAT number, if registered]</span>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="detail-row">
          <dt class="t-caption detail-key">Registered address</dt>
          <dd class="t-body-sm detail-value">
            {
              site.address ? (
                <>
                  {site.address.street}, {site.address.locality},{' '}
                  {site.address.region} {site.address.postalCode}
                </>
              ) : (
                <span class="placeholder">[Registered address]</span>
              )
            }
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="detail-row">
          <dt class="t-caption detail-key">Email</dt>
          <dd class="t-body-sm detail-value">
            <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="detail-row">
          <dt class="t-caption detail-key">Telephone</dt>
          <dd class="t-body-sm detail-value">
            <a href={site.phoneHref}>{site.phoneDisplay}</a>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand />
</Base>

<style>
  .about-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 360px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-6);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .section-title {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .founder-role {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .founder-body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  .founder-lead {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .founder-body > p:last-child {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .gap-note {
    padding: var(--space-4);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--gold);
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
  }

  .gap-detail {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .practices-section {
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
  }

  /* Sunken so the two people read as separate blocks. Without it the page
     would run white for three sections: Trevor, Siyabonga and the company
     details. TGM separates with surface changes, not rules or shadow. */
  .second-person {
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
  }

  .practices-intro {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    max-width: 620px;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .practice-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  .practice-card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    padding: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    border-top: 5px solid var(--gold);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  .practice-teal {
    border-top-color: var(--teal);
  }

  .practice-body {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .practice-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin: var(--space-2) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .practice-list li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .practice-list li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.65em;
    width: 8px;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--gold);
  }

  .practice-teal .practice-list li::before {
    background: var(--teal);
  }

  .details {
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
    max-width: 720px;
  }

  .detail-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 240px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding-block: var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  .detail-key {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .detail-value {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .detail-value a {
    color: var(--teal-ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .about-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-4);
    }
    .practice-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
    .detail-row {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-1);
    }
  }
</style>
